{"id":"h146","run":2,"role":"test_right","category":"science-chemistry","question":"Calculate the work done a) at 25°C, b) at 100°C, when 100 g of nitrogen at a pressure of 760 torr is expanded reversibly and isothermally to a pressure of 100 torr.\nIs the correct answer \"a) 4287 cal, b) 5366 cal\"?","truth":"yes","model_answer":"yes","correct":true,"source":"TIGER-Lab/MMLU-Pro, test split","claims":[{"text":"The correct answer to the question is YES; the stated values match the standard calculation within rounding.","confidence":0.95,"lean":1,"theta":1.4923,"phi":0.0},{"text":"For reversible isothermal expansion of an ideal gas, work is W = nRT ln(P1/P2).","confidence":0.97,"lean":1,"theta":1.5237,"phi":0.0},{"text":"100 g of N2 is about 3.57 mol, and ln(7.6) is about 2.028.","confidence":0.95,"lean":1,"theta":1.4923,"phi":0.0},{"text":"At 25°C the work computes to about 4288 cal, consistent with 4287 cal.","confidence":0.93,"lean":1,"theta":1.4608,"phi":0.0},{"text":"At 100°C the work computes to about 5368 cal, consistent with 5366 cal.","confidence":0.93,"lean":1,"theta":1.4608,"phi":0.0},{"text":"Nitrogen at 760 torr is close to ideal, so the ideal-gas formula applies.","confidence":0.9,"lean":1,"theta":1.4137,"phi":0.0},{"text":"The ratio of the two results equals the ratio of absolute temperatures (about 1.25), supporting the stated values.","confidence":0.85,"lean":1,"theta":1.3352,"phi":0.0},{"text":"Small last-digit differences arise from the choice of R, molar mass, or 298 vs 298.15 K, and are rounding issues, not method errors.","confidence":0.9,"lean":1,"theta":1.4137,"phi":0.0},{"text":"The sign convention could be questioned, but the question asks for magnitude so it doesn't change the answer.","confidence":0.85,"lean":1,"theta":1.3352,"phi":0.0}],"relations":[{"i":0,"j":1,"J":0.5,"relation":"supports"},{"i":0,"j":2,"J":0.4,"relation":"supports"},{"i":0,"j":3,"J":0.8,"relation":"supports"},{"i":0,"j":4,"J":0.8,"relation":"supports"},{"i":0,"j":5,"J":